 to the system can be written as\n\n![](images/36/36b5e6757a7849e9e405883da345dadfbcc3130b517d05e117f4fb6fb4338ae8.png)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139534201 17592186045417 false] [17592186157074 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n![](images/d6/d66051007ab78d2727f3b501156878a369a04b24bca337ed587feb07935a1de7.svg)\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n![](images/bf/bfd809b436c24efcb2e9934b1c36293dfecb4bf87ab41c05f8cbbb9d506ea34d.svg)\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186157074 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/7e/7e191da3b082da420622418962e7491a6e78b6ed8192259b9b50ec8040bbc525.svg)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/fb/fb833263882adc2422083c111e96b0e030263f93e4c0174082f0f57667b46e8e.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/fe/fe33e849a0e3baa7e8547c9559f9c36e980b8e715cba6e26900eea5d815e9b30.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/2c/2c443847a5559737066873593b399117c03e9c8141b403bc7f820c9ad493e2a9.svg)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](images/00/00b635d3858d38c41ad9e1bacb00ed4c6370b5e2112aa82faa6ef37a83209a6c.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/d1/d1911a797bfa5915fb1478c91dc007ca3f1419b1093180dfb08932a008337b6e.svg)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186157074 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/f9/f9f940992f15a14e58f7eee0b8171e8e0e437787d6340618fcd1f23b84d333a8.png)\n\nSince $A$ is upper-triangular, the repeated eigenvalue is $\\lambda=-4$ (of multiplicity two).\nFor this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/9a/9a7426e0e17bb57e1a8bfb24dca5ca3a579809708af3055184f9cfc2728957bc.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/f3/f3e3bae74afa48fd689bb48dc317af52e44914f9f35d32375463d3705268b377.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4+4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4+4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/6e/6e5c9442761d05d62ccc3ce56cbe09794e57b931987e3e4ef33d93d7cfb1e39f.png)\n\nSo, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_2 = 1.\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_1$ is a free variable. Setting $w_1 = 0,$ we get \n![](images/6b/6b54faff854b99c0c86b3de477cc2dde8b5a714bfc296c59ed6757f9bc250f77.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/96/96ee1e3a1af5c670b87cd06e406ac16b0fa9f5e5305b2fe95dc395c079d291da.png)\n\nFinally, we see that $a=0, b=1,$ and $a+b=1.$" 13194139534201 17592186045417 false] [17592186157075 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/94/9459f772e062490c525f6c7bc083c02d0ed5dc3bc4c331f642653aa4f9bf5515.png)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" 13194139534201 17592186045417 false] [17592186157075 1071 "Solve the initial value problem\n![](images/be/be4b1ac82a25533e28028ad0aade71e2dd2614c5cd302d777a90a9f16be845c1.svg)\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda=3.$*" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186157075 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/df/df4b98b4799865dbf40e765bda0fa6176157139d99a62bd1febe48d7c4600c8c.png)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/80/8032d75d584838d0743f2e9217c25844af637d07261dd2d6a89add9df531fdb1.png)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/aa/aa5146c331315952e36051be1126010bbcaf706f2cbc1c886c56b0e92b468fa4.png)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/b0/b0d075668031a0decad6d092f2f03c3f74eacdcff0546e99e8373901c5a910ac.png)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](images/72/726b5737809539337c1a8a259727a47b9bdacdf81de92a4a35783518df9dcaa0.png)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/a1/a1a3e46d8352ceee549fb5040261d8edf036d8fb32fc5fc5b960cab07097970f.png)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/2e/2e3a20e62437546fb2498d1575c9ed2cd268c43b968174ea7e0139977fb6fbd5.png) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534201 17592186045417 false] [17592186157075 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix ![](images/df/dfddfc4e82f21cd490aff75bbdc6fbece98cd05a5cc70a1148492697faa8741a.svg)\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda=3$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n![](images/92/92c6c6be816658e065ebba1079a2c7fa031cc9137ffa8dec7bf6e498e69195f3.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/75/75524e426e894f50985740367c96f128dfef1e216ae6969a529889cf22803bc7.svg)\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda$ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the solution to the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1  \\mathbf{v}e^{\\lambda t} + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}t e^{\\lambda t} +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nTo find $\\mathbf{w},$ we solve the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w} = \\mathbf{v}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-1-3 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 7-3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4 & -8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n![](images/bb/bb892c2fd7b1a2873b9a7427d95599f67fbd10e47ec6fd2cb377b9f6f45f4185.svg)\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\nw_1 =\\dfrac{1}{2} - 2 w_2 .\n$$\n\nThe variable $w_2$ is a free variable. Setting $w_2 = 0,$  we get $w_1 = \\dfrac{1}{2},$ and therefore\n![](images/46/4696fe4ee3fd46a639fb465c10aa2ac44e608a0cf59ca6c1a210eb4861b3dd5b.svg)\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n![](images/05/0525d47967c203832ff4deea0931e007cc7127f24d248cd8265765ad95708367.svg)\n\nNow, we need to find the values of $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial condition ![](images/48/48da1d81a447aa78c3ffa68ad5f089bc0d8e4bac5fa489ddad2747055b0f2a99.svg) Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-2{c}_{1}+\\frac{1}{2}{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the solution to our initial value problem is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{x}(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}-2([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{3t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(4t-3){e}^{3t} \\\\ (1-2t){e}^{3t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186157446 1038 #edb/ref 17592186413832 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186157476 1050 420.0 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186158468 1064 "When solving the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nin the case where $A$ has a single, defective eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the first fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t},\n$$\n\nas usual, and the second fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and $\\mathbf w$ is a vector that satisfies $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nThe reason why we must use this form is that if we were to guess [math]\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}[/math] as a second fundamental solution [math]\\textrm x_2(t),[/math] then we would get\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{x}_2'(t) &= A \\mathbf{x}_2(t) \\\\[3pt]\n\\dfrac{ \\textrm d}{\\textrm dt} \\left[ \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\right] &= A \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t}+ \\lambda \\mathbf{v}  t e^{\\lambda t} &= \\lambda \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t} \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t} &= 0 \\\\[3pt]\n\\mathbf{v} &= 0.\n\\end{align*} \n\nBut we can't have [math]\\mathbf v = \\mathbf 0,[/math] since [math]\\mathbf v[/math] is an eigenvector, and an eigenvector can't be the zero vector! So this guess will not work.\n\nWe can see that the second fundamental solution works by substituting $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{'}(t) & =A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}] & =A(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}) \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Avt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v+\\lambda w & =Aw \\\\ v & =Aw-\\lambda w \\\\ v & =(A-\\lambda I)w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we conclude that $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ works as a second fundamental solution provided that $\\mathbf w$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{v}  =  (A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{w}.$\n\n**Note:**\nBy linearity, any linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ is also a solution of the system. Moreover, it turns out, every solution can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t).$ Later in the course, we’ll see why certain particular solutions can generate the entire solution set." 13194139534201 17592186045417 false] [17592186158468 1064 "When solving the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t),\n$$\n\n \n\nin the case where $A$ has a single, defective eigenvalue $\\lambda$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf v,$ the first fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1(t) = \\mathbf{v}  e^{\\lambda t},\n$$\n\nas usual, and the second fundamental solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{v}$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and $\\mathbf w$ is a vector that satisfies $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nWe can see that the second fundamental solution works by substituting $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ into the differential equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}^{′}(t) & =A{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}] & =A(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}) \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Avt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =\\lambda vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v{e}^{\\lambda t}+\\lambda w{e}^{\\lambda t} & =Aw{e}^{\\lambda t} \\\\ v+\\lambda w & =Aw \\\\ v & =Aw-\\lambda w \\\\ v & =(A-\\lambda I)w.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we conclude that $\\mathbf x_2(t) = \\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t}$ works as a second fundamental solution provided that $\\mathbf w$ is a solution to $\\mathbf{v}  =  (A - \\lambda I) \\mathbf{w}.$\n\n**Note:**\nBy linearity, any linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t)$ is also a solution of the system. Moreover, it turns out, every solution can be written as a linear combination of $\\mathbf{x}_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf{x}_2(t).$ Later in the course, we’ll see why certain particular solutions can generate the entire solution set." 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413826 1080 "-1" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413826 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413826 1083 #uuid "1dfffefc-7dc4-503a-9b1f-a84d08149c77" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413827 1080 "0" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413827 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413827 1083 #uuid "672f41ac-64c5-58b5-868f-7c1ad340ab36" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413828 1080 "1" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413828 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413828 1083 #uuid "9fa05af9-3949-5d3f-84eb-7ccc92942416" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413829 1080 "2" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413829 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413829 1083 #uuid "cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413830 1080 "-3" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413830 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413830 1083 #uuid "fb961936-d8ed-5956-ad00-aabdf66df6d0" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413826 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413827 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413828 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413829 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1075 #edb/ref 17592186413830 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1077 "selection" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1078 #edb/ref 17592186413829 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413831 1079 #uuid "9eaed666-f3ff-5e76-bdcf-86e2bff8289c" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413832 1068 #edb/ref 17592186413831 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413832 1069 "q-80606" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413832 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413832 1071 "Consider the system of linear differential equations given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t).\n$$\n\nGiven that the solution to the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $a+b?$\n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of the system's matrix is $\\lambda =1.$*" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413832 1072 #uuid "9318e8aa-b34c-5ef4-bb58-533fb20cecad" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true] [17592186413832 1074 "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nWe are given that the eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (of multiplicity two). For this eigenvalue, we get \n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n$v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda $ and only one linearly independent eigenvector $v$, the solution of the system of differential equations is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nTo find $w,$ we solve the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A-\\lambda I)w & =v \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2-1 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 4-1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-3 & 9 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1} \\\\ {w}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe last equality allows us to form the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-3{w}_{1}+9{w}_{2}=3 \\\\ -1{w}_{1}+3{w}_{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the first equation is a constant multiple of the second. So, we obtain\n\n$$\n{w}_{1}=3{w}_{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe variable ${w}_{2}$ is a free variable. Setting ${w}_{2}=0,$ we get ${w}_{1}=-1,$ and therefore\n\n$$\nw=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}),\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nFinally, we see that $a=3,b=-1,$ and $a+b=2.$" 13194139534201 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-1dfffefc-7dc4-503a-9b1f-a84d08149c77" 17592186413826 "answer-672f41ac-64c5-58b5-868f-7c1ad340ab36" 17592186413827 "answer-9fa05af9-3949-5d3f-84eb-7ccc92942416" 17592186413828 "answer-cc7ac799-30a1-5efa-892d-6a6142f8760c" 17592186413829 "answer-fb961936-d8ed-5956-ad00-aabdf66df6d0" 17592186413830 "answer-field-9eaed666-f3ff-5e76-bdcf-86e2bff8289c" 17592186413831 "question-9318e8aa-b34c-5ef4-bb58-533fb20cecad" 17592186413832}}
